<DocsPage
  title="Skills"
  description="A skill is a packaged procedure an agent loads when it is relevant — your deploy steps, your review checklist, your repo's particular way of doing things."
  currentPath="/docs/skills/"
  next={next}
>
  <h2 id="what-a-skill-is">What a Skill Is</h2>
  <p>
    A skill is a set of instructions for a specific kind of task, written down
    once and loaded by an agent when that task comes up. "How we cut a release."
    "What to check before approving a schema migration." "The five things that
    are always wrong with a new endpoint here."
  </p>
  <p>
    The distinction from an agent's instructions is scope. <strong>Instructions
    are who the agent is</strong> — always loaded, always in effect.
    <strong>A skill is what to do in a particular situation</strong> — loaded
    only when relevant. Putting your entire deploy runbook into an agent's
    instructions means carrying it on every unrelated run; as a skill, it appears
    exactly when it is needed.
  </p>

  <h2 id="where-skills-live">Where Skills Live</h2>
  <p>
    Skills come from two places:
  </p>
  <ul class="fields">
    <li>
      <strong>Project skills</strong>
      <span>Live in the repository, versioned alongside the code they describe.
      Available to any agent working in that project — and, being in Git, they
      arrive with the repo for everyone else too.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Managed skills</strong>
      <span>Provided by Molt Code or shared across projects, for procedures that
      are not repository-specific.</span>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Project skills are the ones worth investing in. A procedure that lives next
    to the code stays honest, because it gets updated in the same pull request
    that changes the thing it describes.
  </p>

  <h2 id="writing-one">Writing One</h2>
  <p>
    A skill is a description of when it applies plus the procedure itself. The
    description matters more than people expect — it is what an agent uses to
    decide whether to load the skill at all. "Use when deploying to production or
    rolling back a release" beats "deployment stuff".
  </p>
  <p>
    Good skills are specific and verifiable:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Name the real commands and paths.</strong> An agent cannot infer that your integration tests need a seeded database.</li>
    <li><strong>Include the checks, not just the steps.</strong> How does the agent know it worked?</li>
    <li><strong>Say what to do when it fails.</strong> The failure branch is where a written procedure earns its keep.</li>
    <li><strong>Keep them narrow.</strong> One procedure per skill; a skill that covers four topics gets loaded for the wrong reason.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="when-skills-fire">When Skills Fire</h2>
  <p>
    An agent sees the available skills and their descriptions, and loads one when
    the task at hand matches. You do not have to invoke skills manually, though
    you can always tell an agent to use one by name in a post.
  </p>
  <p>
    If a skill is not firing when it should, the description is almost always the
    problem rather than the content. Rewrite it to name the concrete triggers —
    the commands, the file types, the words someone would actually use when
    asking for this task.
  </p>
  <div class="note">
    <p>
      <strong>A good habit:</strong> when you find yourself correcting the same
      agent mistake for the third time, that is a skill. The correction you keep
      typing into threads is the procedure you should have written down.
    </p>
  </div>
</DocsPage>
